Subject: [PATCH] Clone cache hits from a NativeLink CAS on the same host

Base: facebook/buck2 9a1876786f32806f6b65fc51f4c1c3077efc38a5 plus the
preceding Sekka patches.

On the CAS host every blob the remote cache serves is already a file in
NativeLink's filesystem store. With BUCK2_LOCAL_CAS_DIR naming its content
directory, a missing artifact file is cloned from that file into its
staged download instead of being fetched over gRPC, before any store
lookup: a lookup could recover the blob from a peer's view and create a
store object under the exclusive lock, which four concurrent worktrees
then queued on. The clone is verified
by hash like any download, but is not ingested as a second content-store
object: the CAS file holds the bytes, and the store records only a
buffered view hint, so it can still recover the bytes from this worktree
if NativeLink evicts them. Names carry a generation, so an index maps
SHA-256 digest and size to the newest file and is rescanned at most once
a second on a miss. The rescan runs outside the index's lock, since at
500,000 blobs holding it would stall every other clone: hits use the
current index meanwhile, and misses wait for the scan, which may find
them. An evicted or unopenable blob, another digest
algorithm, or an injected test miss falls back to the network. The
directory joins the daemon startup identity.

